  <div class="hero-qa">
    <div class="qa-item">
      <h3 class="qa-question">Why Theory of Space?</h3>
      <p class="qa-answer">
        Existing evaluations of foundation models predominantly measure passive spatial reasoning: models infer relations or answer questions from fixed observations, curated views, or pre-collected trajectories. This leaves a critical gap for embodied intelligence, where perception is inherently partial and useful spatial knowledge must be built through self-directed exploration. An agent must decide what to observe next to reduce uncertainty, integrate egocentric observations over time, and maintain a globally consistent spatial belief about both seen and unseen structure. Theory of Space is needed to evaluate this missing capability directly. We provide an evaluation paradigm that isolates exploration from reasoning, measures belief utilization and exploration efficiency, and probes the model’s internal spatial belief and uncertainty—going beyond downstream task accuracy to diagnose where spatial belief construction fails.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="qa-item">
      <h3 class="qa-question">What is the challenge:</h3>
      <p class="qa-answer">
        The core challenge is that embodied spatial intelligence requires active decision-making under partial observability, not merely processing more observations. An agent must identify what is unknown in its current belief, choose actions that maximize information gain, and know when to stop exploring once uncertainty is sufficiently reduced. This demands integrating egocentric, sequential views into a globally consistent spatial belief about both seen and unseen structure, and updating that belief when the environment changes outside the agent’s view. Naive strategies such as random wandering or exhaustive sweeping are inefficient and often fail to resolve ambiguity. The challenge is amplified in vision: small perceptual errors can cascade into global inconsistencies and unstable beliefs, making reliable belief construction and uncertainty management difficult beyond downstream task accuracy.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="qa-item">
      <h3 class="qa-question">What do we do?</h3>
      <p class="qa-answer">
        We introduce Theory of Space, an evaluation paradigm that tests whether foundation models can construct, update, and utilize spatial beliefs through active perception under partial observability, in both text-based and vision-based environments. Specifically:
      </p>
      <h4 class="qa-subtitle">Belief utilization via a full spatial task suite.</h4>
      <ul class="qa-list">
        <li>Route-level (egocentric, path-based) belief: PAIRWISE RELATION (DIR), PERSPECTIVE TAKING (PT), ACTION-TO-VIEW (A2V), PERSPECTIVE DECISION (PD), and VIEW-TO-ACTION (V2A).</li>
        <li>Survey-level (allocentric, map-like) belief: ALLOCENTRIC MAPPING (AM), MENTAL ROTATION (MR), LOCATION2VIEW (L2V), and VIEW2LOCATION (V2L).</li>
        <li>Belief Updating: FALSE BELIEF (FB) under covert environment changes.</li>
      </ul>
      <h4 class="qa-subtitle">Active–passive protocols to disentangle exploration from reasoning.</h4>
      <ul class="qa-list">
        <li>Active agents choose actions (move/rotate/observe/terminate) to reduce uncertainty.</li>
        <li>Passive comprehension agents reason from standardized logs generated by scripted proxy explorers, isolating exploration strategy from belief inference.</li>
      </ul>
      <h4 class="qa-subtitle">Direct probing of internal belief and uncertainty beyond answer accuracy.</h4>
      <ul class="qa-list">
        <li>Models externalize a structured cognitive map and uncertainty over unobserved structure.</li>
        <li>We evaluate exploration efficiency (information gain / uncertainty reduction per step) and belief quality (consistency and stability), enabling diagnostic analysis beyond downstream task scores.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="teaser-figure">
